{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Wehrmacht (] , lit.","title":"Wehrmacht"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" "defence force") was the unified armed forces of Nazi Germany from 1935 to 1946.","title":"Wehrmacht"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It consisted of the "Heer" (army), the "Kriegsmarine" (navy) and the "Luftwaffe" (air force).","title":"Wehrmacht"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The designation "Wehrmacht" for Nazi Germany's military replaced the previously used term, "Reichswehr" (1919–35), and was the manifestation of Nazi Germany's efforts to rearm the nation to a greater extent than the Treaty of Versailles permitted.","title":"Wehrmacht"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"General Friedrich Kühn (7 August 1889 – 15 February 1944) was a General der Panzertruppe in the Wehrmacht during World War II.","title":"Friedrich Kühn"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was a recipient of the Knight's Cross of the Iron Cross of Nazi Germany.","title":"Friedrich Kühn"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Canadian Armed Forces (CAF; French: "Forces armées canadiennes" , "FAC"), or Canadian Forces (CF) (French: "Forces canadiennes" , "FC"), are the unified armed forces of Canada, as constituted by the National Defence Act, which states: "The Canadian Forces are the armed forces of Her Majesty raised by Canada and consist of one Service called the Canadian Armed Forces."","title":"Canadian Armed Forces"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The XV Mountain Corps was a German military formation that commanded German forces conducting counter-insurgency operations against the Yugoslav Partisans in the Independent State of Croatia during World War II.","title":"XV Mountain Corps (Wehrmacht)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was formed in the Balkans from the staff of the German commander in Croatia (German: "Militärbefehlshabers in Kroatien" ) on 12 August 1943.","title":"XV Mountain Corps (Wehrmacht)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was subordinated to the 2nd Panzer Army, and from its formation until 1 November 1943 the corps was commanded by "General der Infanterie" (Lieutenant General) Rudolf Lüters, and between 1 November 1943 and July 1944 it was commanded by "General der Infanterie" Ernst von Leyser, who was replaced in August 1944 by "General der Panzertruppe" (Lieutenant General) Gustav Fehn who commanded the corps for the rest of the war.","title":"XV Mountain Corps (Wehrmacht)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In May 1944, the corps was responsible for the conduct of Operation Rösselsprung, which was aimed at killing the Partisan leader Josip Broz Tito.","title":"XV Mountain Corps (Wehrmacht)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The corps surrendered to the Partisans on 8 May 1945, and Fehn and many of the remaining men of the corps were subsequently killed by the Yugoslav authorities.","title":"XV Mountain Corps (Wehrmacht)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"General der Panzertruppe (Literally: General of the Armoured Corps) was a General of the branch OF8-rank rank of German Army, introduced in 1935.","title":"General der Panzertruppe"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A "General der Panzertruppe" was a Lieutenant General, above Major General (Generalleutnant), commanding a Panzer corps.","title":"General der Panzertruppe"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Battle of the Argenta Gap was an engagement which formed part of the Allied spring 1945 offensive during the Italian Campaign in the final stages of the Second World War.","title":"Battle of the Argenta Gap"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It took place in northern Italy from 12–19 April 1945 between troops of British V Corps commanded by Lieutenant-General Charles Keightley and German units of LXXVI Panzer Corps commanded by Lieutenant General ("General der Panzertruppe") Gerhard von Schwerin.","title":"Battle of the Argenta Gap"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Wehrmacht
